{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_g5ccqmhdvuwmotxgn6amnyqhlc2zh73v3knibgjud5oiel6ks4qq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases are complete and the gate passes from clean builds.\n\n## What I found\n\nThe translation was already correct on every observable output path — but it had **one real defect**, in exit status rather than text:\n\n**SIGPIPE disposition.** The Rust standard library installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` before `main` runs; a C program starts with the default. So when stdout's reader disappeared, the C program was *killed by signal 13* while the Rust program swallowed the `EPIPE` (the `p!` macro correctly discards write errors, mirroring the C never checking `printf`) and exited `0`:\n\n```\nC: returncode=-13     R: returncode=0\n```\n\nFixed with a `reset_sigpipe()` call as the first statement of `main`, restoring `SIG_DFL` via a bare `extern \"C\" { fn signal(...) }` — no new dependency. This was the only change I made to the Rust program.\n\n## A caveat worth flagging\n\nMy first version of the SIGPIPE test was itself racy and I nearly shipped it. It fed ~18 KB of output, which fits in a 64 KiB pipe buffer, so whichever process won the scheduling race could finish before the reader closed. It failed 7 of 25 full-suite runs — **in both directions** (`C(13) vs Rust(0)` and `C(0) vs Rust(13)`), which is what proved the test was at fault rather than the translation. The fix (~3 MB of output, far more than a pipe holds) makes the outcome forced: 30/30 clean runs, while still failing 5/5 when I remove `reset_sigpipe()`.\n\nI also caught that an earlier stability check I ran was worthless — `cargo test ... dead_stdout_reader -- --exact` silently matched zero tests, so \"0 failures / 40\" measured nothing. I re-ran it asserting on `1 passed`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **19 tests, ~340 inputs**, each asserting stdout, stderr, *and* exit code + signal. Plus 3 000 ad-hoc random inputs during investigation — 0 mismatches.\n- **Mutation-tested the suite** so it isn't vacuous: five injected defects (dropped SIGPIPE reset, altered error string, `%.2f`→`%.3f`, `fgets` limit `size-1`→`size`, saturating instead of truncating the `int`) were all caught, then reverted.\n- Notable C behaviours confirmed replicated: `fgets` splitting a 300-byte line across iterations; glibc's `%d` saturating at `LONG_MAX` then **truncating** to `int`, so `4294967302` genuinely runs \"all demos\" and `2^64+1` gives `Invalid choice`; the 255-byte buffer boundary; `°`/box-drawing bytes; NUL handling in fixed-width fields.\n- Four C branches (`size == 0` guards, `found == 0`, `find_expensive_items`) are **unreachable from `main`** since the demos use fixed data — recorded in `ERRORS.md` rather than left as an untested gap.\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified (source mtimes unchanged); CMake builds go out of tree into `target/c_build` when no in-tree build exists. Full write-up in `translation/ERRORS.md`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1631,"cache_read_input_tokens":99902,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1631,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0a3d7b95-e4e3-4db5-b2bb-1298e0d0e457","uuid":"0d7a53f3-e7e3-4f70-aa3a-a4978ebf6be8","timestamp":"2026-08-28T01:26:24.249Z"}
